COMPLETE INITIAL LISTING SCHEDULER, MARKET LAUNCH & MARKET MAKER PREPARATION SYSTEM

1. ROLE AND OBJECTIVE

Act as a Senior Centralized Exchange (CEX) Architect, Backend Engineer, Trading Engine Engineer, Database Architect, and Frontend Developer.

You are working on an existing project named SilentSmile Exchange.

The application already has an exchange UI, market selector, simulated spot trading, order book, candlestick chart, order engine, simulated wallets, admin panel, and Market Maker Bot components.

Your assignment is to implement a complete, reliable, database-backed Initial Listing Lifecycle System.

Do not rebuild the project from scratch.

First inspect the existing repository, identify the current architecture, and reuse the existing components, types, storage, authentication, trading engine, wallet, ledger, and UI wherever possible.

The final result must provide a coherent workflow from creating a market pair through scheduled listing, pre-launch MM preparation, public trading launch, and delayed public deposit/withdrawal availability.

All assets, balances, deposits, withdrawals, and trades must remain simulated. However, every virtual financial operation must be recorded and reconciled. A virtual balance must be real within the simulator's accounting model, not an arbitrary number displayed by the UI.

Do not connect to real customer funds or real blockchain withdrawals.

2. NON-NEGOTIABLE BUSINESS RULES

Implement these rules throughout the system:

Creating a market pair does not automatically list it.

Approval does not automatically make a market publicly visible.

An approved market must have an explicit listing schedule before it can become an upcoming listing.

An upcoming market must not accept public orders.

MM funding access and MM order-placement access must have separate opening times.

MM preparation orders must use actual recorded virtual inventory and the existing CLOB infrastructure.

Public users must not execute trades against pre-launch MM orders.

At the scheduled listing time, the system must verify operational readiness before opening public trading.

Valid pre-launch MM orders must be preserved when the market launches.

The public Spot interface must display an upcoming listing and countdown before launch.

A listing that is not live must never appear as an ordinary active trading market.

The NEW badge must be separate from the market's trading status.

Public trading, deposit, and withdrawal must have independent status and permission checks.

Reaching a countdown of zero must never be sufficient by itself to authorize a financial operation.

Rescheduling, pausing, postponing, or cancelling a listing must invalidate or reconcile obsolete scheduled actions.

Every administrative state change and virtual financial transfer must be auditable.

No fake fills, fake order-book liquidity, fabricated balances, or fictitious trade volume are allowed.

Every setting exposed in the admin UI must either work or be explicitly marked as not implemented.

